  "body": "The dashboard update button **corrupted the install on Windows**. PATCH `4.21.1` → `4.21.2`.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`uv tool install --force` removes the existing tool environment before reinstalling. But `fcc-server` *runs from that environment* — on this machine, `…\\uv\\tools\\free-claude-code\\Scripts\\python.exe`.\n\nWindows holds a running executable and its loaded DLLs open. The removal fails partway with the access-denied error you saw, leaving a **half-deleted environment** — hence the corruption. WSL and Linux were unaffected because POSIX unlinks open files without complaint, which is exactly why it worked for you there.\n\n## Fix\n\nWindows now **stages** instead of installing in place:\n\n1. Download the wheel and verify its SHA-256 — unchanged, same checks as before.\n2. Write it to `~/.fcc/updates/`.\n3. Spawn a **detached PowerShell helper** that waits for this process to exit, pauses for Windows to release the handles, runs `uv`, and records the outcome.\n4. The banner reads *\"Update staged — stop the server to finish installing\"*.\n\nYou stop `fcc-server`, the update applies by itself, you start it again on the new version.\n\n**The existing install is never touched while it's running**, so a failed update can no longer strand you mid-way — the old version stays fully working until the moment it isn't running.\n\n## Why PowerShell, not Python\n\nThe only interpreter we can rely on is the one **inside the environment being replaced**. Using it would hold open the very directory `uv` needs to delete — reintroducing the bug in a new place. PowerShell is always present on Windows and is external to the target.\n\nArguments are passed as a fixed argv, quoted for PowerShell, so release metadata reaching the wheel name can't inject commands. Spawned `DETACHED_PROCESS | CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP` so it survives both this process and the console the server was started from, and it gives up after an hour rather than sitting resident forever.\n\n## Reporting a failure you can't see happen\n\nThe helper only reports *after* the server that staged it is gone. `ReleaseStatus` now carries the recorded outcome, so a failed deferred install surfaces in the dashboard on the next start rather than vanishing silently.\n\n## Verification\n\nTests assert the helper waits before installing, quotes hostile arguments, that Windows **never invokes `uv` in-process** (the stub raises if it tries), and that the detach flags are set. The generated script was also **run for real** against a harmless command and produced the expected result file.\n\nExercised end to end against the live release feed:\n\n```\nok      : True\nmessage : 4.21.1 is verified and staged. Windows cannot replace the environment\n          while the server is running, so stop fcc-server to finish installing…\nlog     : downloaded free_claude_code-4.21.1-py3-none-any.whl\n          verified sha256 f2670a3cb38f3af0…\n          staged for install after shutdown (Windows)\nhelper  : powershell.EXE -NoProfile …\nflags   : 0x208\n```\n\nThree existing in-place tests now pin `_WINDOWS=False` so they no longer depend on the host OS — they were silently taking the Windows branch on a Windows runner.\n\nFull suite passes except the 14 failures that also fail on unmodified `main`.\n\n## Residual risks\n\nThe helper is fire-and-forget: if the machine is powered off between staging and shutdown, the staged wheel simply sits in `~/.fcc/updates/` and the install never runs. Harmless, but it does mean \"staged\" isn't a guarantee — the next status check will still show an update available.\n\nIf someone stops the server and immediately restarts it within the helper's 2-second settle window, the install could still race the restart. Unlikely by hand; worth knowing if you script it.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
