    "message": "fix(updates): defer the Windows install until the server exits\n\nThe dashboard update button corrupted the install on Windows. `uv tool install\n--force` removes the existing tool environment before reinstalling, but\nfcc-server runs from that environment: Windows holds the running python.exe and\nits loaded DLLs open, so the removal fails partway with an access-denied error\nand leaves a half-deleted environment behind. WSL and Linux were unaffected\nbecause POSIX unlinks open files without complaint.\n\nWindows now stages instead of installing. The wheel is downloaded and its\nSHA-256 verified exactly as before, then written to ~/.fcc/updates and handed to\na detached PowerShell helper that waits for this process to exit, pauses for\nWindows to release the handles, runs uv, and records the outcome. The dashboard\nsays the update is staged and to stop the server to finish; the existing install\nis never touched until it is no longer running, so a failed update can no longer\nstrand anyone mid-way.\n\nThe helper is PowerShell rather than Python because the only interpreter we can\nrely on is the one inside the environment being replaced -- using it would hold\nopen the very directory uv needs to delete. Arguments are passed as a fixed argv\nquoted for PowerShell, so release metadata reaching the wheel name cannot inject\ncommands. It is spawned DETACHED_PROCESS | CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP so it\noutlives both this process and the console the server was started from, and\ngives up after an hour rather than sitting resident forever.\n\nBecause the helper reports only after the server that staged it has gone,\nReleaseStatus now carries the recorded outcome and the dashboard surfaces a\nfailed deferred install on the next start instead of losing it.\n\nVerification: tests assert the helper waits before installing, quotes hostile\narguments, that Windows never invokes uv in-process (the stub raises if it\ndoes), and that the detach flags are set; the generated script was also run for\nreal against a harmless command and produced the expected result file. Exercised\nend to end against the live release feed: downloads 4.21.1, matches the\npublished digest, stages it, and spawns powershell with flags 0x208. Three\nexisting in-place tests now pin _WINDOWS=False so they no longer depend on the\nhost OS. Full suite passes except the 14 failures that also fail on unmodified\nmain.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>",
